--- a/docs/INSTALL.md
+++ b/docs/INSTALL.md
@@ -284,5 +284,25 @@ journalctl --user -u droidloom.service -n 100 --no-pager
 journalctl -b -u droidloomd.service -n 200 --no-pager
 ```
 
+`droidloomctl status` reports the owned cell lifecycle. While the cell is running,
+it does not run Android readiness probes or claim that Android is ready; normal
+exited-cell polling and cleanup still apply. Use `droidloomctl readiness` for one
+fresh Android service observation, or `droidloomctl wait` to wait for readiness.
+Start and restart still wait for Android unless `--no-wait` is explicit. Callers
+that previously used Status as a readiness gate must move to Readiness together
+with the matching daemon and CLI. Do not treat a lifecycle response as a ready
+result or fall back to it when Readiness fails.
+
 These commands do not require sudo. Replace the example package in crash reports
 with the failing app. See [KNOWN_ISSUES.md](KNOWN_ISSUES.md) for current limits.
+
+### Pocket4 production compatibility
+
+The Status/Readiness integration retains the installed native media backend
+(drm_media_dma_heap): only the real system DMA heap is added to the private
+guest device tree, with mode 0444. Host device permissions are never changed.
+The outer namespace owner keeps the production TERM child and parent-death
+contract; Android init's separate SIGKILL parent-death protection is unchanged.
+The media implementation is preserved from production source 43c1cfc087361e9a4e5599500da1f2b8249bf38f
+plus its recorded native-media patch. The initial 500dd3-based Status candidate
+without these production features is not an installation candidate.
